8,8,8,8,8,4,2,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,8,6,8,9,8,8,8,8,8],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,2,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,8,6,8,9,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,8,6,8,9,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid contains.** The grid has two kinds of non-background things.\n\n- **Devices.** These are 2-cell-thick bars. Each bar is made of two parallel lines.\n  - The **far line** has a *doubled* end: its last two cells are the same colour. Examples are `4 3 3 3 3 4 4` in pair 1, `4 4 2 2 2 2 2 2 4` in pair 2, and `1 1 2 2 2 2 3` in the test.\n  - The **near line** is the other line. Examples are `2 2 2 2 2 2 4` and `1 1 1 …`.\n  - Rays always leave from the near-line side.\n- **Markers.** These are single isolated cells. Each marker sits out on the near side of exactly one device, lined up with one position along that device.\n\n**Evidence.**\n- In pair 1, the marker 5 at (25,3) is below device B, whose bar spans cols 1–7 with its doubled end at col 7. Col 3 is index 4 counted from the doubled end.\n- In every device's output, colour 5 is drawn at index 4 from that device's doubled end.\n- The same holds for marker 6, which is index 1 from device D, and for marker 1, which is index 5 from device C.\n- Pair 2 gives the same picture: 3 is at index 1, 6 at index 3 and 9 at index 5, for all three devices, whatever their orientation.\n\nSo each marker defines a mapping *index → colour*. That mapping is then stamped onto **every** device.\n\n**The rule.** For every device and every index in the mapping, shoot a ray of the mapped colour. It starts from the near line at that index, goes outward perpendicular to the bar, and continues to the grid border over background cells.\n\n**How markers are assigned.** A marker belongs to the device it lies beyond on the near side, within that device's span. This settles the ambiguous cases. In pair 2, marker 6 at (0,19) lies in the same row as device 1, but it is on device 1's far side. Within its span and beyond its near side, it belongs only to device 2.\n\n**The test input.**\n- It has four devices. Each far line is `1 1 2 2 2 2 3` and each near line is `1 4 4 4 4 4 3`.\n- Marker 9 at (1,0) lies left of the vertical device in cols 4–5. Its near side is col 4.\n- Marker 7 at (11,28) lies right of the device in cols 24–25.\n- Marker 6 at (28,21) lies below the horizontal device in rows 2–3.\n- Each marker yields an index. All four devices then get rays of colours 9, 7 and 6 at those indices.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits one canvas after the rays of each device are drawn, then emits the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True)\n    devices, markers = [], []\n    for o in objs:\n        if o[\"size\"] == 1:\n            markers.append(o[\"cells\"][0])\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        if (y1 - y0) == 1:  # horizontal bar\n            lines = [[(y0, x) for x in range(x0, x1 + 1)], [(y1, x) for x in range(x0, x1 + 1)]]\n        else:\n            lines = [[(y, x0) for y in range(y0, y1 + 1)], [(y, x1) for y in range(y0, y1 + 1)]]\n        far = None\n        for li, line in enumerate(lines):\n            v = [g[p] for p in line]\n            if v[0] == v[1] != v[2]:\n                far, rev = li, False\n            elif v[-1] == v[-2] != v[-3]:\n                far, rev = li, True\n        if far is None:\n            continue\n        near = lines[1 - far]\n        if rev:\n            near = near[::-1]\n        fy, fx = lines[far][0]\n        ny, nx = lines[1 - far][0]\n        d = (ny - fy, nx - fx)  # outward direction\n        devices.append((near, d))\n\n    # build index -> colour mapping from markers\n    mapping = {}\n    for (my, mx) in markers:\n        for near, (dy, dx) in devices:\n            found = False\n            for i, (py, px) in enumerate(near):\n                if dy != 0 and px == mx and (my - py) * dy > 0:\n                    found = True\n                elif dx != 0 and py == my and (mx - px) * dx > 0:\n                    found = True\n                if found:\n                    mapping[i] = g[my, mx]\n                    break\n            if found:\n                break\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for near, (dy, dx) in devices:\n        for i, c in mapping.items():\n            if i >= len(near):\n                continue\n            y, x = near[i]\n            y += dy; x += dx\n            while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and (g[y, x] == bg or g[y, x] == c):\n                out[y, x] = c\n                y += dy; x += dx\n        emit(out, \"Shoot the marker-defined rays outward from the near side of one device.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every device carries the same coloured rays.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That mapping is then stamped onto **every** device."}],"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Rays always leave from the near-line side."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The rule.** For every device and every index in the mapping, shoot a ray of the mapped colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"All four devices then get rays of colours 9, 7 and 6 at those indices."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a9336502415abcf4acf4a3bf8255bb4b0b10f8bf784080a0853a09778e96215f","task_sha256":"a1e1f51f8f0c6568d2c4ed470e6041cd542993efc9d3caea29d22675f020d6f4","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
